 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "They may be using **different scales or time windows**, so the labels don’t necessarily conflict.\n\nIf by **PSI** you mean Singapore’s index, it’s a **24-hour average** across six pollutants; “Moderate” is 51–100 and “Unhealthy” starts at 101. An app may instead show a more current PM2.5 reading, use a different country’s AQI scale, or pull data from a different nearby monitor. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?trk=article-ssr-frontend-pulse_little-text-block))\n\nIn the U.S. AQI, for example, **101–150 means “Unhealthy for Sensitive Groups”**—a label that can be shortened or displayed differently by an app. Readings can also vary between monitors, especially when smoke or pollution is patchy. ([epa.gov](https://www.epa.gov/wildfire-smoke-course/communicating-air-quality-conditions-air-quality-index))\n\nTo compare them, check that both are for the **same place and time**, and look at the **number, pollutant, and averaging period**—not just the label. If you share the app’s reading and what PSI source you’re looking at, I can help reconcile the exact numbers.",
